{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["Aiah and Anah — it appears to me thus: mules, for the word כופסי means 'and she conceived,' and similarly in the case of Shet. And even though in Chronicles it is written: Aiah and Anah, one need not be precise about this, just as it is written in the Torah עוץ וחול וגתר ומש, while in Chronicles ומשך והמפרש, the extra vav. One may wonder what reason there was to write an extra vav here more than for the other sons. And the proofs that they bring are not valid, for those vavs that they cite are not extra: ותשב תמר ושוממה — the meaning is like: שבי אלמנה בית אביך, for the word 'sitting' is a doubling of expression for 'desolate,' so it is as if it were written: ותשב בדודה ושוממה. מגערת אלהי יעקב נרדם ורכב וסוס — this is its meaning: At the rebuke of the God of Jacob, the might of our enemies mentioned above was put to sleep, and also his rider and his horse, as it is written: סוס ורוכבו רמה בים." "He is Anah — who is mentioned in the days of Moshe, on account of his might." "Who found the yemim — the mighty ones, or wild beasts, and defeated them. According to its plain sense one need not be more precise than this."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "The lemma 'Aiah and Anah' does not appear in Genesis 36:24; the verse contains only ענה. Rashbam may be addressing a variant reading or a related verse (cf. Genesis 36:2). The comment includes multiple proof-verses (Ruth 1:11, Psalm 76:7, Exodus 15:1) with no addresses given, and the second half appears to be a separate excursus on extra vavs; the connection between the sections is not fully clear in the transmitted text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:46:25.956889758Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}